00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:00.950 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  satellite images from this year show how  china is expanding its military arsenal  from missile launch sites in remote  deserts to aircraft carriers  and submarines at sea  china's buildup in recent years has been  unprecedented while china's military  spending is much lower than the us its  defense budget has more than doubled  over the past decade  the build-up is aimed at meeting  beijing's goal of having a world-class  military by 2049 the 100th anniversary  of the founding of the people's republic  of china china perceives the us  increasingly as a threat and wants to  find ways of countering the united  states which operates as the premier  power broker in asia  the wall street journal analyzed recent  satellite images from maxar technologies  and planet labs and spoke to military  experts who studied them and they reveal  how beijing is advancing its nuclear and  naval arsenal to take on the us and its  allies  china successfully tested its first  nuclear weapon in the 1960s and since  then it's developed a few hundred  warheads according to u.s government  estimates that's far less than the  roughly 4 000 the u.s has  however in the past year or so beijing's  stance has shifted the chinese  leadership has decided that it needs a  lot of nuclear weapons compared to what  it had before hans christensen has been  studying china's nuclear development for  more than four decades and is currently  with the federation of american  scientists a non-profit policy think  tank based in washington dc  satellite images show china's been  adding what appears to be missile launch  facilities in the remote deserts of  central and western china zoom in and  you see this they put up these big  inflatable domes over the silo  construction sites both to protect  against climate but also to protect  against um you know spy satellites and  things like that when the basic  construction is done the inflatable  domes are taken down revealing silo  fields these contain huge canisters that  are built into the ground and can be  used to house nuclear-tipped missiles on  this elevated dirt mount christensen  says there appears to be a silo hatch  which is a lid to a missile canister  and there's also a wide road that could  be used to transport missiles on trucks  in the past two years or so christensen  has observed more than 300 silos under  construction across china up from some  20 the country used to have before 2018.  and he says the primary focus of these  silos would be to expand china's nuclear  arsenal china's development indicates  that it's sort of it has begun to think  that it is more vulnerable to attack  than it was before  military analysts say one of these sites  is large enough for a new long-range  chinese missile which can carry nuclear  warheads and is capable of reaching the  us mainland while the chinese government  hasn't officially confirmed or denied  the construction of these miso silo  fields beijing has pledged not to use  nuclear weapons unless provoked  china is also beefing up its navy that's  critical because about one third of the  country's borderline faces the ocean  at the southern edge of hainan island is  a chinese navy base  this satellite image shows two of  china's nuclear-powered ballistic  missile submarines  military analysts say it's the only  vessel in the chinese navy dedicated to  launching nuclear weapons it's china's  first credible sea based deterrent  matthew funiole is a senior fellow with  the center for strategic and  international studies his team obtained  these satellite images from maxar  technologies in order to analyze china's  military development funioli says china  is relatively new to deploying such  submarines which are capable of  launching ballistic missiles that can  reach as far as 5 600 miles and the  chinese military says each submarine  could carry up to 12 of them in the  region itself you know the jail 2 could  certainly strike india could strike  russia but if you're thinking about  whether or not it could strike targets  across the continental united states um  not not so much  another significant upgrade to china's  naval fleet a newer aircraft carrier  this satellite image shows a shipyard in  shanghai and since at least 2018 china's  been building what military analysts say  will be the country's biggest aircraft  carrier the new vessel is bigger than  its two predecessors and this extra  space means a longer and wider flight  deck for larger and more powerful  aircraft to take off what that gives you  is that you can generate a lot more lift  to try to get heavier airframes into the  air so more fuel and a lot of cases  bigger aircraft and do so at you know up  tempo at you know at faster speed while  china has revealed little information  about the aircraft carrier funioli says  satellite images show that the vessel  could be equipped with an advanced  launch and recovery system like the one  used by u.s and french aircraft carriers  with this design a catapult launches the  aircraft  and during landing arrester wires catch  it the pentagon expects the vessel to be  operational by 2024 and when deployed  china will be able to project power  farther away from the mainland it gives  china a flexible tool to sail your  aircraft carrier fleet through a  strategic waterways way of sending a  signal to other regional powers  to prepare for potential conflicts with  other military powers satellite images  also showed china constructing what  appears to be mock-up models in the  shape of a u.s vessel in the desert  military analysts say the chinese army  is increasingly focused on creating  realistic training environments and it's  not just against us troops but also  other adversaries  for instance this satellite image shows  what appears to be a surveillance plane  in the desert and the aircraft looks  like one that's operated by the japanese  air force military experts say the size  and shape particularly the radar mounted  on the back of the plane indicate that  it's a japanese aircraft  china's ministry of national defense  didn't respond to a request for comment  on questions about the country's  military buildup  security experts say china's expansion  has been across a range of assets  because it's about looking beyond the  u.s as its only challenge china has a  very broad  horizon when it comes to potential  adversaries that is looking you know  almost 360 degrees around uh china those  adversaries include close us allies like  japan and south korea which are also  increasingly casting a wary eye on china  so as beijing continues to invest in its  arsenal and military technology security  experts say it's also pushing the us to  stay competitive to defend against any  potential threats from its rival

